    "narrative": "The core of our lineup today is anchored by **Corbin Carroll**, who commands the highest score of the day (84.80) as he squares off against the vulnerable Junk. With a favorable park factor and a massive boost from a positive platoon alignment, **Corbin Carroll** is our primary engine for both power and the high-value stolen bases we desperately need. **Mickey Moniak** also emerges as a high-upside play at 75.75, benefiting significantly from a +14% park factor boost and a favorable matchup that maximizes his potential for production. Meanwhile, **Seiya Suzuki** (73.90) and **Josh Naylor** (74.13) both provide robust value against starters with high expected ERAs, serving as reliable pillars to keep our efficiency rating high across the board.\n\nThe most difficult decision on our bench involved **Austin Riley**, whose 51.51 score was the highest among our reserves but ultimately wasn't enough to displace our starters. Despite a decent park factor against Gausman, his subpar BvP history and the daunting quality of the opposing arm caused the optimizer to favor the more favorable matchups of **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Isaac Paredes**. We are also keeping a close eye on the speed threats: while **Brice Turang** and **Jose Caballero** aren't putting up elite base scores, their matchups against pitchers with slow deliveries keep them relevant for potential theft opportunities.\n\nStrategic caution is warranted given the variability in our lower-tier starters. **Moises Ballesteros** and **Zach McKinstry** are hovering near the 46.0 mark, sitting just above our \"below floor\" threshold; they remain active primarily because of their specific lineup placements. We have officially benched **James Wood** due to his absence from the active lineup, and we are monitoring the status of **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Max Muncy**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Travis Bazzana** to ensure we maintain our game cap discipline as we head into the final stretch of the season.",
